          Executive Summary: Homeowners Insurance Trends
        </div>
        <ul class="callout-list">
          <li><strong>National Average Premium:</strong> A standard policy with $350,000 in dwelling coverage averages <strong>$2,151 a year</strong> nationwide. In storm-heavy states like Florida, Louisiana, Texas, and Oklahoma, typical bills run $3,800 to $5,600.</li>
          <li><strong>Replacement Cost &ne; Market Value:</strong> Don't set your dwelling limit off your purchase price or a Zillow estimate. Market value bakes in land and school districts; your insurer only pays for lumber, roofing, and contractor labor to rebuild the house itself.</li>
          <li><strong>Watch Out for ACV Roof Clauses:</strong> More carriers are downgrading roofs older than 10 to 15 years from Replacement Cost Value (RCV) to Actual Cash Value (ACV), which can leave you paying thousands out of pocket after a hailstorm.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <p class="lead-text">
        Between pricier building materials, tight contractor schedules, and back-to-back hail and wind seasons, homeowners insurance bills have climbed fast. Knowing what your HO-3 or HO-5 policy actually pays—and where the hidden exclusions sit—keeps you from overpaying on renewals or getting blindsided after a storm.
      </p>

      <h2>The Six Standard Coverages Inside Every HO-3 Policy</h2>
      <p>
        Most single-family homes are insured on an ISO HO-3 (Special Form) policy. It splits your protection into six buckets:
      </p>

      <div class="table-responsive">
        <table class="data-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Coverage Section</th>
              <th>What It Covers</th>
              <th>Standard Policy Formula</th>
              <th>Typical Limit ($350k Home)</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Coverage A: Dwelling</strong></td>
              <td>The physical structure: framing, roof, drywall, foundation, built-in cabinetry</td>
              <td>100% of estimated replacement cost</td>
              <td>$350,000</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Coverage B: Other Structures</strong></td>
              <td>Detached garages, tool sheds, perimeter fences, gazebos, driveways</td>
              <td>10% of Coverage A</td>
              <td>$35,000</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Coverage C: Personal Property</strong></td>
              <td>Furniture, electronics, clothes, appliances (worldwide protection)</td>
              <td>50% to 70% of Coverage A</td>
              <td>$175,000 – $245,000</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Coverage D: Loss of Use</strong></td>
              <td>Additional living expenses (hotel, rental, dining) while your home is uninhabitable</td>
              <td>20% to 30% of Coverage A</td>
              <td>$70,000 – $105,000</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Coverage E: Personal Liability</strong></td>
              <td>Lawsuits for bodily injury or property damage you or family members cause to others</td>
              <td>Selected limit ($100k min; $300k–$500k standard)</td>
              <td>$300,000 – $500,000</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Coverage F: Medical Payments</strong></td>
              <td>Minor medical bills for guests injured on your property regardless of legal fault</td>
              <td>Fixed limit ($1,000 to $5,000 per person)</td>
              <td>$5,000</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
