      <h2>Replacement Cost vs. Market Value: Why They Rarely Match</h2>
      <p>
        First-time buyers often assume their dwelling limit should equal their mortgage or appraisal price. In practice, those two numbers can sit hundreds of thousands of dollars apart:
      </p>

      <div class="card" style="margin: 1.5rem 0;">
        <h3 style="color: var(--color-primary); margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">Why Market Value Differs from Dwelling Cost:</h3>
        <p>
          Say you buy an older home outside Boston for <strong>$850,000</strong>. Here is what you're actually paying for:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>The Land Value:</strong> Dirt doesn't burn down or blow away. In high-demand metros, the lot alone can make up 30% to 50% of the sale price ($250,000–$400,000).</li>
          <li><strong>Location & School District:</strong> A top school zone drives up resale prices, but it doesn't change the cost of framing lumber, copper pipe, or drywall.</li>
          <li><strong>Actual Rebuild Cost:</strong> Hauling debris, pulling permits, and rebuilding that 2,200-square-foot house to current local code might cost <strong>$420,000</strong>. Paying to insure it for $850,000 just inflates your annual bill for coverage you can never collect.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <h2>Three Endorsements Most Homeowners Forget to Add</h2>
      <p>
        Standard homeowners policies leave a few nasty coverage gaps that catch people off guard mid-claim. Three inexpensive add-ons close the biggest ones:
      </p>

      <h3>1. Water Backup & Sump Overflow Endorsement</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Standard Policy Rule:</strong> Base HO-3 policies exclude water backing up through city sewers, floor drains, or a failed sump pump.<br />
        <strong>The Fix:</strong> Adding a water backup rider runs about <strong>$35 to $75 a year</strong> and gives you $10,000 to $25,000 for soaked basement drywall, flooring, and furnace repairs.
      </p>

      <h3>2. Ordinance or Law (Building Code Upgrade) Coverage</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Standard Policy Rule:</strong> Standard coverage only pays to put your house back the way it was before the loss.<br />
        <strong>The Fix:</strong> If your city updated electrical, energy, or wind-strap codes since your home was built, bringing a repaired house up to modern code can tack on an extra $20,000. A 25% or 50% Ordinance or Law rider covers those mandatory upgrades for around $40 a year.
      </p>

      <h3>3. Extended or Guaranteed Replacement Cost</h3>
      <p>
        After a major wildfire, tornado, or hurricane hits a region, contractor labor and material prices often jump 30% or more overnight. An <strong>Extended Replacement Cost rider</strong> adds a 25% to 50% buffer on top of your Coverage A limit so a post-storm price spike doesn't leave your rebuild half-finished.
      </p>

      <h2>Percentage Deductibles vs. Fixed Dollar Deductibles</h2>
      <p>
        Flip to your declarations page and check for "split deductibles." In hurricane, tornado, and hail states, insurers frequently carve weather losses out into their own steeper deductible:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Standard All-Peril Deductible:</strong> A flat dollar amount (like $1,000 or $2,500) for fire, theft, vandalism, or burst pipes.</li>
        <li><strong>Named Storm / Hurricane Deductible:</strong> Calculated as a percentage of Coverage A (usually 1%, 2%, or 5%). On a $400,000 dwelling limit with a 2% hurricane deductible, you pay the first <strong>$8,000</strong> before the insurer writes a check.</li>
        <li><strong>Wind & Hail Deductible:</strong> Standard across much of Texas, Oklahoma, Kansas, and Nebraska, typically set at 1% to 2% of your dwelling limit.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Actionable Checklist to Lower Your Home Insurance Costs</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Bundle Home and Auto:</strong> Pairing your home and car policies with one insurer is still the biggest single discount on the table, averaging 15% to 22%. See the carrier-by-carrier math in our <a href="/guides/home-auto-bundle/" style="color: var(--color-accent); font-weight: 600; text-decoration: underline;">home and auto bundling discount guide</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>Install Monitored Fire & Burglar Alarms:</strong> A UL-certified alarm tied to a central monitoring station typically earns a 5% to 10% credit.</li>
        <li><strong>Upgrade to Class 4 Impact-Resistant Shingles:</strong> In hail country, putting on Class 4 shingles when you replace your roof can cut the wind/hail portion of your bill by up to 25%.</li>
        <li><strong>Raise Your All-Peril Deductible:</strong> Stepping up from a $1,000 deductible to $2,500 usually trims 12% to 15% off your annual premium—and discourages small claims that hurt your renewal rate anyway.</li>
        <li><strong>Audit Your Limits Every Three Years:</strong> Check that your dwelling estimate keeps up with local rebuild costs, and trim excess personal property limits if you've downsized.</li>
      </ol>

