      <!-- FAQ Section -->
      <div class="faq-section">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions About Homeowners Insurance</h2>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-question">Does homeowners insurance cover flooding? <span>+</span></button>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            No. Standard homeowners policies (HO-3, HO-5, and HO-6) exclude rising groundwater, overflowing rivers, storm surge, and surface runoff. To cover flood damage, you need a separate policy through the federal National Flood Insurance Program (NFIP) or a private flood insurer.
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-question">What is the difference between an HO-3 and an HO-5 policy? <span>+</span></button>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            An HO-3 covers your house itself on an "open perils" basis (everything is covered unless explicitly excluded), but covers your personal belongings only for 16 listed perils like fire, theft, and wind. An HO-5 extends open-perils coverage to both the building and your belongings, which is worth pricing out if you own higher-value furniture, gear, or electronics.
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-question">Should I file a small home insurance claim? <span>+</span></button>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            Usually not. Say a repair costs $1,800 and your deductible is $1,000—the insurer only pays $800, yet that claim sits on your C.L.U.E. loss history for 5 to 7 years. One small payout can easily trigger a 20% to 40% rate hike or even a non-renewal notice.
          </div>
        </div>
